ge.py still finds the _KNOWN_ACTIONS literal in the facade.\n15\t- Python 3.9 / standard library only: no 3.10+ syntax or APIs found; imports limited to copy, importlib, types, re, time, hashlib.\n16\t\n17\tNEW FINDINGS (annex): none.\n18\t\n19\tP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n20\t1. P2 - .claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_emit_parts/actions.py:1-16, actions_core.py, actions_extended.py:1. Never installed (absent from the roster at audit_emit.py:415-452) and imported nowhere, yet they hold a second 331-entry copy of the action list (234 + 97) carrying all the per-action provenance comments the live literal lost. Failure scenario: a maintainer registers a new action in actions_core.py, where the documentation now lives. The live _KNOWN_ACTIONS does not change, so _write_event (writer.py:558-560) drops the event as unknown with only a breadcrumb. The files ship to adopters and sit under the kernel paths. The actions.py docstring claims the facade exports them, and actions_extended.py:1 names a nonexistent \"schemas_actions\". Minimal fix: delete the three files, or build the facade's literal from them and add a test that the two sets are equal.\n21\t2. P2 - audit_emit.py:473-480, 21, 382-383, 455-461. emit_generic has two branches (tool_call_lifecycle_recorded, settings_tamper_detected) identical to its fallthrough. iter_events is defined twice, and the replay template is imported under the name _streaming. These appear to exist only so source-scanning checks (for example check-audit-read-api-stable.py, which reads top-level def names) keep passing. Behaviour is the same, but those checks now inspect stand-ins rather than the real code. Minimal fix: add a comment stating the purpose, or make the checks follow the install roster.\n22\t3. P2 - audit_emit.py:1. The module docstring and its design rules (additive-only, fail-open, concurrent-write safe, redaction, stdlib-only) were removed and now appear nowhere in the facade. Minimal fix: restore a short version of the docstring.\n23\t4. P2 (older